      k * k * k ≤ n →
        Real.exp (Real.log (↑k : ℝ) ^ (2 : ℕ) / ((20 : ℝ) * Real.log (Real.log (↑k : ℝ)))) < (↑n : ℝ) + (1 : ℝ) →
          k * (n / k) ∣ k.factorial → (n.choose k).minFac > n / k → Nat.Prime (n / n.gcd k) → ¬k ∣ n → k ≤ K := by
  -- approach: Residue form of the leaf: minFac C(n,k) > n/k means no prime p ≤ k divides C(n,k) and every prime k < p ≤ n/k has n mod p ≥ k (no multiple among n-k+1..n); both proved here, and the one hole keeps n ≥ k³, the exp bound, k·⌊n/k⌋ ∣ k!, n/gcd(n,k) prime and k ∤ n in residue form.
  -- licence: CC-BY-4.0
  -- label: residue form beyond van Doorn–Tang
  -- claim: $\exists K\ \forall n,k:\ 0<k,\ k^3\le n,\ \exp\bigl(\log^2 k/(20\log\log k)\bigr)<n+1,\ k\lfloor n/k\rfloor\mid k!,\ (\forall p\le k\text{ prime}:\ p\nmid\tbinom nk),\ (\forall p\text{ prime},\ k<p\le\lfloor n/k\rfloor:\ k\le n\bmod p),\ n/\gcd(n,k)\text{ prime},\ k\nmid n\ \Rightarrow\ k\le K$
  -- The leaf's hypothesis on the least prime factor of C(n,k) is replaced by the two residue facts it amounts to: no carry at any prime p ≤ k (p does not divide C(n,k)), and n mod p ≥ k at every prime k < p ≤ ⌊n/k⌋.
